WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Kabuki, Quantum Fighter, Earth, the Future.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:29.200
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.960
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:32.360
And I'm Joe.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.960
And was the back of the box just like random words?

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.880
Yeah, you're right. It's like Earth, the future.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:46.800
I don't have the back of the box present right now, but I know I specifically picked that just to fool around with the back of the box.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:51.840
There was a lot more like plot details, but then it was just one sentence that said Earth, the future.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.220
And I was like, yeah, let's fucking go with that.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.480
Like, that sounds awesome.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.260
So it's not really either the beginning or the end of the back of the box.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.040
It was in there, but I picked it.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:02.180
And that's the rules.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.220
You're allowed to do that. You should just pick like a bunch of random letters and rearrange them into whatever words we want to say.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.600
Yeah. I mean, is anybody really ready for the future?

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:14.220
Not 2054.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.740
2056. Come on, Joe.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.900
I'm not ready for that. Joe, Jesus Christ.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.320
Did you even play the game?

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:22.520
I played the prequel.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.720
It's so 2056 coded that I don't know how you missed that.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.980
Yeah, but it is Kabuki Quantum Fighter.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.320
It's 2056.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:35.100
A rogue virus has hijacked the planet's defense supercomputer.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:38.260
Interesting that it's just supercomputer.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.880
Like, there's just one. Well, it's the supercomputer.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.900
Like, the rest of them are just like regular computer.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.400
What happened? Did one company win?

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.300
Like, right now, we're in a state where, like, every company is building their own supercomputer or data center or whatever.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:54.400
Is there going to be?

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.700
So, there would be data centers, but this is just supercomputer with no S.

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:09.860
Well, basically, over enough time, and I guess the 30 years until whatever year that was, is enough time for them all to sort of grow together.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:11.980
And then here's the problem.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.980
That defense supercomputer, it's the one controlling the nuclear weapons.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.160
It didn't even have a password.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.320
Right. Yeah, no nuclear codes.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.980
The nuclear weapons themselves are being controlled by the computer.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:30.120
And it seems like basically all everything, they've run out of options.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:43.740
So, they're going to upload you, the player character's consciousness, directly into this machine as binary data and fight the virus from the inside.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:50.100
This kind of sounds a little relevant to what's going on today, right?

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.600
Like, we're a couple of years away.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:56.880
Maybe it doesn't even take until 2056 before we start uploading consciousness into the clawed to fight back.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:26.860
Ugh.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.760
It's got a little bit more nuance than that.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.020
I appreciate that.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:32.280
Yeah, I liked it, too.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.960
I mean, I can't pretend that it's not just one giant exposition dump.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.520
But, like, I was invested.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.760
You know, it was, you know, it got me.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:47.380
And the player characters is Colonel Scott O'Connor.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:49.720
Scott Connor.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.440
That's kind of, Sean, you mentioned the Terminator reference.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:53.460
That's kind of close.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:57.580
Yeah. To the Connors of the Terminator universe.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.260
And when he enters, like, how does it work?

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.000
Does his mind enter the computer?

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.060
Is he physically in the computer?

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.160
I think. Does he know how to control the body?

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.940
I don't think he's physically in there. Isn't he in that, like, he's, like, laying in that.

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:14.740
He's in that. Yes, he's laying in that thing.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.560
He was, like, an image of his brain was made.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:26.960
Right. And his, like, consciousness has been copied into the computer and is now doing the thing.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:31.300
Do you think all of our consciousnesses have, like, that hair?

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.360
Yeah, let's go.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.060
Now, here's the interesting thing.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:44.500
Did you know that when he entered that system, the system used a image of his great-great-grandfather,

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:47.380
who was a Japanese kabuki actor?

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:55.640
And so your digital avatar fights with the traditional costume, white face paint, and extremely long red hair of a traditional kabuki actor?

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:01.300
And they do bring this up in that intro movie, and they just sort of, like, yeah, that's normal.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.480
Like, oh, wasn't... Yeah, that'll happen.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.480
Wasn't his great-great-great-grandfather doing that?

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:11.480
Yeah, does that mean that this guy's great-great-grandfather was, like, somehow just, like, deeply embedded in this guy's subconscious?

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:16.360
Or was it just, like, no, no, it's like an Assassin's Creed thing where, like, your DNA has memory?

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:18.720
Was that a big thing in Ireland?

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.140
I think it's the Assassin's Creed thing. In what?

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:24.000
Was kabuki, like, really big in Ireland?

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.800
Why Ireland? His name is O'Connor.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:29.860
Oh, right, right, right. Yeah, that's good.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.660
Very good. But wait, yeah, no, of course.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.000
Kabuki, do they, like, fight with their hair?

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:39.580
No, never. But they just happen to have long hair.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:44.320
Yeah, yeah. Because, honestly, the, like, fighting with your hair thing, we didn't mention it, but you fight with your hair.

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:48.780
Yeah, he's bayonet. It's so silly and also so cool to me at the same time.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:53.840
I love it. It actually is convincing, too, like, the animation for it and everything.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:59.760
Like, I believe that those whips of the hair are doing significant damage, at least in a digital world.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:03.400
It should be doing damage to yourself as well, though, like, with the whiplash.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:07.900
Yeah, but when it's your digital body, you don't have to worry about your digital bones.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.440
Is there not, like, digital CTE?

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.320
There actually probably is, right?

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:14.800
But who's simulating that?

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:18.140
Is it the AI? Because why can't the AI just simulate him feeling bad?

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:25.060
Yeah, the AI should just be, like, running the sadness program on him as a defense.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.800
And, like, this is not realistic.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:33.660
I think that the AI is trying, like, it's, you know, this is all it trying to get a hold of this program so it can run whatever it wants to.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:37.540
It's like a, it's like your computer trying to locate a virus, you know?

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.960
Right, right. I think that's what all these enemies are, are parts of the AI trying to do it.

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:42.840
So don't worry, we got that covered. They're the white blood cells.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.120
Yeah, yeah. Yeah.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.200
This was developed by Human Entertainment.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:53.520
We always like to call them out because that's just, it's perfect when they make especially games about AI and call themselves Human Entertainment.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:59.060
But published by HAL Laboratory, you know, the Kirby and Smash Brothers people.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:05.480
So I don't really know if it has any of that DNA, but we've seen some other games from them before.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:17.980
I think, you know, the manual and certainly, like, everything we've been talking about so far commits hard to this, like, science of consciousness being uploaded to an AI framework.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:21.840
And then, as Joe mentioned, the avatar fights with his hair.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.860
So there's, like, this gap between the game's pretensions and its reality.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.140
And I think that's actually where the charm comes from because the gameplay itself is actually kind of fun.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:38.540
As a result, it's just really silly that the plot is so serious, but the game is actually kind of whimsical.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:47.300
Yeah, but I also think that the seriousness of the plot can sometimes be read as, like, over-the-top, like, cool.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.460
You know, over-the-top cool in the sense where it's almost a parody.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:53.100
I know it's not, but it feels that way.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:57.500
Like, I feel like if it were made today, it would be parodying games like this.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:08:04.740
So, like, I kind of read it as, like, oh, this is, like, so fun because it's, like, trying to be so crazy serious and it's not.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:11.240
It's kind of like how, you know, the costume design in The Matrix was cool.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:18.940
And now, whenever you see somebody like that, they're usually, like, dancing to, like, goth electronica under a bridge.

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:20.820
Right, right.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:33.040
And, you know, I think it's obviously done on purpose that it's mixing a very old art form in Kabuki with a futuristic outlook with AI and uploading your consciousness into a machine.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:37.280
And even the title, like, Kabuki Quantum next to each other is pretty crazy.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:41.440
You seem to be a little worried about the consciousness angle.

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:44.020
I don't think I'm worried about it.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:57.160
I think it's, like, just a neat approach to then, like, to take a very traditional, exclusive to Japanese art form of Kabuki theater and then, like, bring that over to America.

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:58.940
Like, they surely could have.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:00.860
They didn't have to keep that part.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:02.620
They could have localized it a little bit.

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:05.620
Yeah, I'm surprised that they didn't change the...

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.140
Because everything else is so sci-fi.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.420
And then you just got the sky.

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:24.880
The coolest thing about maybe the aesthetic of the game, though, is that before you even take control of the character and start playing the game, the game opens on a wall of text of what looks like real assembly code.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:48.680
You know, showing instructions of the game, the game opens on a wall of text of what looks like real assembly, the game opens on a wall of text of what looks like real assembly.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.680
But I don't...

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:57.580
You see it a lot more now, like, with, like, glitchiness as just an aesthetic choice.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.580
And I wasn't expecting something like that.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:03.000
So it was a cool touch.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.080
I appreciated it.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.800
And I'm certainly no expert on Kabuki theater.

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:14.580
But outside of the main character, I don't think there's any other part of the identity of that in this game.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:22.000
So I'm sure that the face paint and the clothing is all accurate to the Kabuki time period.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:24.560
But otherwise, that's it for the game, right?

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:31.340
Yeah, I guess we'd have to zoom in a bit to tell if it was period accurate.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:33.680
No, yeah, sure.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:39.940
But even just the idea of it, it's only in the way that the character looks.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:45.140
Right. Not even in, like, the enemies or the...

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:47.860
It doesn't feel like you're part of a stage play.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:53.280
Right. In Kabuki theater, that's the one where, like, there's the percussion, right?

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:54.760
Masks. Like, really, like... Yeah.

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:59.660
But right, there's that, like, really, like, I don't know, that distinct sounding percussion, right?

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:05.480
Yes, that's true. It would have been nice if there was, like, a character theme that had that or something.

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:11.780
And then the environments themselves, I wouldn't say they don't look nothing like a computer,

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:15.720
but they're certainly not what I would think the inside of a computer would look like.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:19.800
It looks more like a biological corruption of a computer.

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:23.120
Like, there are living, breathing parts of this thing.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:25.140
There's one screen where there's just a bunch of...

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:28.280
Not just one, but a bunch of beating hearts and stuff.

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:29.880
It looks like...

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.200
Or, you know...

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.140
It's very alive.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:40.980
And maybe that's on purpose, but you would expect maybe more circuit boards and things of that, not body horror machinery.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:44.260
Like, yeah. It kind of jumps back and forth between stages.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:48.780
And some of them are even just like, oh, it's just all bones.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:50.380
All bones everywhere.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:55.080
But, again, like, I don't think that the original...

00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:58.820
That this game, like, was supposed to be about this originally.

00:11:58.820 --> 00:12:00.600
Like, the Japanese version, at least.

00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:02.920
So I'm just supposed to give it a pass?

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:07.740
No, I'm saying that, like, it just makes sense that it doesn't do that.

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.300
See, you got it. Yeah, and I, like...

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.620
Because I almost... I wouldn't say that I wouldn't...

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:15.800
Necessarily that I wouldn't expect all the biological stuff.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:17.360
It's that... Or that I would expect...

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:21.000
I'm sorry. I wouldn't say that I would expect there to be more circuit boards.

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:23.800
It's that I would expect there not to be the biological stuff.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:25.220
Because there are circuit boards.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:26.320
And it does feel like that.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:27.220
But then, yeah, it's like...

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:31.120
It's almost like the computer was corrupted by an alien.

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:33.520
Or something. Ooh.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:34.820
Yeah, I mean...

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.560
It looks more like dark fantasy than cyberpunk.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:45.180
But maybe it was a deliberate metaphor for a biological virus entering the AI.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:47.580
You know, maybe the...

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:52.500
Maybe even though it wasn't intentional, as Sean's trying to point out, it could be interpreted that way.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:57.300
So to get into the combat, we have, obviously, the hair-based combat.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:01.460
Oddly, though, when you crouch, you don't then, like, crouch and whip your hair.

00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:02.880
You resort to your punches.

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.160
So you are capable of punching.

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:06.900
You are capable of dealing damage that way.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.680
You just choose to attack with your hair.

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:12.700
What is the range like?

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:15.160
Well, the hair is way better than punching.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:16.040
For sure.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:17.620
Which, it bothered me a little bit.

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:19.200
Like, I just want to use my hair when I'm crouching.

00:13:19.280 --> 00:13:20.500
But I wonder if the punching is in there.

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.580
Have you tried head-binging while you're crouching?

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:23.720
In real life?

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.280
Yeah. This is what makes it realistic.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:29.080
No, that... I can head-bing while I crouch.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:30.480
That's the only time I do it.

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:38.120
But I... Yeah, the only reason I could think of that they would put the punching when you're crouching

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.460
is maybe to make you appreciate the reach of the hair.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:42.440
And be like, oh, yeah, see, that's why we're using the hair.

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:43.740
It's so much better than the punching.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:45.780
Yeah, it feels crunchier, too.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:46.880
There's a little bit of a delay.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:50.140
Or at least you kind of have to let the animation play out.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:58.140
And that, coupled with the fact that it does more damage, at least it feels like a better tactic.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:03.700
And your other button is for jumping, which, of course, there are platforms and such.

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:09.420
But there are also hooks built into the walls, I guess.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:10.580
Maybe they're floating.

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:11.940
It's a computer.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:13.440
Anything can happen, I suppose.

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:17.720
There's these hooks that you can grab onto, and they allow you to swing.

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:22.840
And you can just tap A for a short swing or hold down for a full arc.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:27.900
That gets a little tricky, especially when there's a lot of hooks and a long way to fall down.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:34.740
I noticed that sometimes I would just instinctually, when I grabbed onto the next hook, begin to start swinging from it

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:38.540
and then leap off of it, most likely into an enemy that I didn't intend to.

00:14:39.380 --> 00:14:45.000
But there is also, you know, he has a little bit of that Ninja Gaiden wall climbing ability as well.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:51.220
You'll see more hook swinging than you will wall climbing, but they're both present and spice up the platforming.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:58.380
And it's not to say that these stages are, you know, they're more horizontal side scrollers than they are vertical,

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:01.480
but they do scale up a lot.

00:15:01.480 --> 00:15:07.160
And the idea of, like, having branching paths isn't there, but there are, like, different ways to approach.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:09.700
There isn't always just the one way up.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:14.760
Yeah, and you think it's a hot take for me to say that I think the way these hooks work

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:21.060
is how I wish the grappling hook worked in Bionic Commando.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:24.780
Ooh. Like, I just feel like this is how I want that to feel.

00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:26.440
Yeah, no, it is smooth.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:27.600
It just feels smooth. Yeah.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:31.720
And it, like, makes you feel pretty badass the whole time.

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:34.780
Like, I'm never, like, oh, I got to stop and think, how does this work again?

00:15:34.780 --> 00:15:38.600
It's just kind of, like, it's just kind of intuitive after you kind of get the hang of it.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:43.280
Yeah, I think the one thing I wasn't ready for, though, is just, like, letting go of the A button

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:46.220
when you land on the next one in order to just hang there.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:50.960
Like, for some reason, because you're holding down A from the swing and you're releasing,

00:15:51.120 --> 00:15:55.460
when I get to the next one, my instinct is to then press A again to, like, confirm it.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.500
Not to, you know, not to let go of it.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:07.980
There was some times where I just unintentionally fell to damage or death because I was still getting

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:10.300
the hang of it. But over time, you get the hang of it.

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:15.400
And, you know, there's a lot of, I don't want to say random, but there's a lot of drops

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:19.760
in the vertical sections of this game where you kind of have to do a little bit of a leap

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:25.600
